<id/00/0046/#concept> a <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E55_Type>, skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Peas not fresh other than Split Peas"@en ;
    skos:broadMatch <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q29472543> ;
    skos:broader <id/sub/sub_pulses_leg_wd_q29472543/#concept> ;
    skos:definition "(Ger. erbsen; Fr. pois; Ital. piselli, pisi; Span. pesoles, guisantes; Russ. goroch). The pea is one of the most esteemed of the leguminous or pulse plants. It is supposed to be indigenous to the south of Europe, and was cultivated by the Greeks and Romans, the latter of whom probably introduced it into Britain. There are many varieties; but the common garden pea (*Pisum sativum*) and the common grey or field pea (*Pisum arvense*) are the most generally cultivated; being reared in large quantities in all parts of the country, particularly in Kent. But since the introduction of the drill husbandry the culture of the pea as a field crop has been to a considerable extent superseded by the bean. Sometimes, however, it is drilled along with the latter; [J. R. McCulloch, Dictionary of Commerce and Commercial Navigation (1880), scan p. 1152]"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "Curated classification (processing_state=raw_unprocessed): Processing state: whole nuts/kernels/seeds/peas are unprocessed; classifier matched 'expressing Oil', 'Ground (nuts)' or 'Split'. Layer-1 observations: 518 rows, 1903–1940. Series: HCPP_Annual Statement of Trade (1910-1940 ext) ×451; HCPP_Annual Statement of Trade ×67. Units: CWTS (Hundredweights) ×518. Top routes: New Zealand → United Kingdom ×35; Other Foreign Countries → United Kingdom ×35; Netherlands → United Kingdom ×35; World → United Kingdom ×34."@en ;
